{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>The sons of Jacob came, etc.</b> The meaning is all of them together. Scripture intends to state the reason for the plundering of all the city's property, etc. — for the shame touches all of them, and each one must receive compensation commensurate with his shame. This is as the Rambam wrote (Hilchot Na'arah Betulah 2:5), and this is his language: The court evaluates his standing and her standing, and they assess how much money is fitting for her father and her family to pay so that such a thing would not happen to them from this man, etc., and he is obligated to pay this amount — end quote. And the brothers are included in the family. Therefore they gathered together to estimate the amount befitting their shame, and they plundered the entire city, and it was not sufficient to match their shame; and they took everything to the credit of Jacob their father, for it concerned him. And even though they judged them with a death sentence, we do not say concerning children of Noah קים ליה בדרבה מיניה (Tosafot, Eruvin 62a, s.v. Ben Noach)."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "The abbreviation וגו׳ at the end of the lemma corresponds to the verse's continuation עַל הַחֲלָלִים וַיָּבֹזּוּ אֶת הָעִיר. The Talmudic phrase קים ליה בדרבה מיניה means literally 'he is held by the greater [punishment] of it' — i.e., once liable to death, no further penalty is extracted — left untranslated as a technical legal idiom."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:03.688600857Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}